WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.190
Speaking of this extraordinary book that I've been reading

2
00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:07.340
and I will forever have the chart

3
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:11.257
that Dr. Perry put in the book, as far as-

4
00:00:11.257 --> 00:00:12.500
The the upside down triangle?

5
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.880
The upside down triangle.

6
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.880
what you guys talk about as far as the impression

7
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.730
that children are left with at such an earlier age

8
00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:27.730
than I think was spoken about in at least my growing up.

9
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:31.920
I think even into almost having kids,

10
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.490
I feel like everybody kind of went,

11
00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.884
they won't notice when they're young.

12
00:00:36.884 --> 00:00:41.250
And I did not understand why that was the notion.

13
00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.500
It didn't seem right, it didn't feel right,

14
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.410
but I wanted to believe it

15
00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.740
because that's what so many people had said.

16
00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:48.770
Here's the bottom line

17
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:53.290
that really was the hugest epiphany for me.

18
00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:58.120
all these years, I've done multiple shows where people say

19
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.250
exactly what you just said, Drew.

20
00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.824
Oh, they won't remember.

21
00:01:01.824 --> 00:01:04.230
Yes, I'm in an abusive relationship.

22
00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.910
And as soon as, you know, the kids are young,

23
00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:09.590
when they get older, I'm gonna move,

24
00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:11.090
I'm gonna get out of the relationship.

25
00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:15.000
The most damage is done from zero to two months

26
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.820
'cause that's the time when the brain

27
00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:18.430
is taking in everything.

28
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:20.100
And anybody who's ever had children,

29
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.610
you all know this because you get to watch that whole

30
00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.130
language socialization process.

31
00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.780
How did they learn the word dada?

32
00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.110
How did they learn mama?

33
00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:30.500
How did they learn spoon?

34
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:31.410
How did they learn?

35
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.480
So as they're taking in all of that

36
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.980
when the brain is the most open and receptive,

37
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:40.110
they're also taking in all the other stuff.

38
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.360
That changed everything for me,

39
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.150
in terms of how people are raising their kids

40
00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:49.880
and how we see that what you do in front

41
00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.300
of the youngest children matters.

42
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:56.084
You know, I have a question, which is that,

43
00:01:56.084 --> 00:02:00.400
when I was born, my dad, he had split with my mom.

44
00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.430
And I just didn't know him, he was very abusive,

45
00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.923
He would come around, he would trash the house.

46
00:02:08.109 --> 00:02:09.043
He was scary.

47
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.090
And you know, there are a lot of people

48
00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.710
and you share a story of what happened

49
00:02:17.710 --> 00:02:21.490
the first night that you stayed with your mom in Milwaukee.

50
00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.190
And for...

51
00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:27.330
people who have had a parent,

52
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.370
one or two that did not want to be in their child's lives

53
00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.570
for whatever their reasons were, which is valid to them,

54
00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:37.270
very hard for a child to understand.

55
00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:40.420
What do you talk through with yourself

56
00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:43.960
or other people who have had that kind of start

57
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.050
to their lives, who deal with that kind of trauma?

58
00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:49.330
You talk about the eyes

59
00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:52.510
and the being wanted and the being set up right.

60
00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.960
What happens when you're not?

61
00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.120
So here's the good news about all of this,

62
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:00.770
because all of us have had things

63
00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.600
to happen to us in our lives

64
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:08.490
that caused us to feel unwanted

65
00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:13.360
and not important.

66
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:15.450
Everybody has at some point in your life,

67
00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.780
even if you come from the best of circumstances.

68
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:23.270
It is possible to take your post-traumatic pain

69
00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.760
and turn that into post-traumatic power

70
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.870
and post-traumatic wisdom.

71
00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:33.660
I know this for sure that whatever has happened to you

72
00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.270
can also be for you

73
00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:38.800
if you allow it to be.

74
00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.960
The thing that has healed me the most

75
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.770
is taking the pain that I endured, suffered,

76
00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:49.900
survived as a child and turning that inside out.

77
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.200
So that is why I created my school

78
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.450
specifically looking for girls who are just like me,

79
00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.760
who came from poor backgrounds,

80
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.210
who came from dysfunctional backgrounds,

81
00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:03.450
who many times felt unwanted, unloved,

82
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.690
how do I create an environment for them?

83
00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.530
How do I give back?

84
00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.040
This is how you save yourself,

85
00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:13.590
how do I give back that which I was not given?

86
00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.230
And when you can do that,

87
00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:17.760
you begin to fill the hole inside yourself.

88
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.760
That giving back has become really what my legacy is.

89
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:24.730
And I will share this with you

90
00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.090
that I was sitting with Maya Angelou

91
00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.610
after I'd come back from the opening of my school.

92
00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.120
And I said, "Oh, this is gonna be,

93
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.680
the school, this is gonna be my greatest legacy.

94
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.660
I mean, these girls, this is what my life was for."

95
00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:41.070
And she said, "You have no idea what your legacy will be."

96
00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:42.920
And I said, "Oh, no, no, no, no, Maya,

97
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:43.990
you she should have been there,

98
00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:46.020
I'm telling you, it's gonna be the school."

99
00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.730
And she said, "I said you have no idea

100
00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.670
what your legacy will be.

101
00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:56.830
Your legacy is every life you touch,

102
00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.930
your legacy isn't your name on a school

103
00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:01.850
or any other building, your legacy is every life you touch."

104
00:05:01.850 --> 00:05:03.690
So I say this to you, Drew Barrymore,

105
00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.570
your legacy, this is what Maya said to me.

106
00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:07.340
Everybody who's ever watched a show

107
00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.920
and decided I'm gonna go back to school.

108
00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:11.210
I'm gonna go on a diet.

109
00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.630
I'm gonna treat myself better.

110
00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:14.920
I'm gonna leave an abusive relationship.

111
00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:18.250
Every life that you have touched

112
00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.290
that has experienced your voice

113
00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:24.933
and the power of your platform, that is your legacy.

114
00:05:24.933 --> 00:05:27.760
(audience cheers)

115
00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:30.080
And that is why in Oprah, we trust.

116
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:33.977
And I just wanna say also that you said,

117
00:05:33.977 --> 00:05:35.947
"Here's the good news."

118
00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.890
That to me is one of the most positive and powerful ways

119
00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.080
in which to engage people's minds and hearts

120
00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.333
because it is so hopeful.

121
